.\" Manual page for sicil. Keep it in step with sicil/__main__.py and README.md.
.TH sicil 1 2026-11-06 "sicil" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sicil \- check whether installed packages have known security problems
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sicil
.RB [ \-h ]
.I command
.PP
.B sicil run
.RB [ \-h ]
.RB [ \-\-db
.IR path ]
.PP
.B sicil report
.RB [ \-h ]
.RB [ \-\-all ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-db
.IR path ]
.RI [ filters ]
.PP
.B sicil show
.RB [ \-h ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR field ]
.RB [ \-\-db
.IR path ]
.I id
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sicil
checks once a day whether the packages installed on a CachyOS or other
pacman-based system have known security problems.
It combines what it finds with how recently
.BR yoklama ,
a separate package usage recorder, saw each package used,
and sorts the result by what is worth attention.
.PP
.B sicil
only reads and reports.
It never installs, updates or removes a package, needs no root,
and writes nothing to
.BR yoklama .
.PP
Its report is a hint, not a verdict; see
.BR CAVEATS .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B run
Read the sources, record what changed in the database and print a short summary:
whether there is a new problem, what changed, and where the kernels stand.
This is the command the daily timer runs.
It creates the database if there is none and upgrades an older one.
.IP
A source that fails skips only its own part of the check.
The previous state of that part is kept, none of its findings are closed,
and the report says the run was incomplete.
A new red finding raises one desktop notification,
a single summary if there are several;
nothing else notifies, and the same finding does not notify twice.
.TP
.B report
Print the recorded findings.
Each row starts with an ID to use with
.BR "sicil show" ,
and its description sits below it.
.IP
The database is opened read-only and the network is not used.
The only program it may run is
.BR vercmp (8),
for the version filters.
On a terminal the output fits the width:
descriptions are cut to one line, and a table too wide drops its least
important columns and says which.
In a pipe nothing is shortened.
A column empty in every row is left out,
and a value shared by every row moves above the table.
.IP
The header warns if the last run is more than 36 hours old or was incomplete,
and carries the warning of
.B yoklama
about a short observation period.
.TP
.B show
Print one record, judged against the version installed now.
The full description is shown here, without the cut
.B report
makes on a terminal.
See
.BR IDS .
.IP
The database is opened read-only and the version comes from
.BR \%pacman (8);
the network is not used.
For a kernel the record is read from the local checkout.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help and exit.
Works on
.B sicil
and on each command;
.B sicil \-h report
is the same as
.BR "sicil report \-h" .
.TP
.BI \-\-db " path"
Use the database at
.I path
instead of
.IR \(ti/.local/state/sicil/sicil.db .
Accepted by every command, after the command name.
The kernel checkout is kept beside whichever database is named,
so this also tries a run without touching the real state.
.SS Options of report
.TP
.B \-\-all
Also show closed findings.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print JSON, with the complete lists, including every CVE covering a kernel.
Filters apply to it too.
The top-level keys are
.BR generated_at ,
.BR last_run ,
.BR warnings ,
.BR yoklama_found ,
.BR red ,
.BR yellow ,
.BR removal_suggestions ,
.BR unverified ,
.BR looks_closed ,
.B kernels
and
.BR not_found ;
.B closed
is added with
.BR \-\-all ,
and
.B filtered
with any filter.
.SS Options of show
.TP
.BI \-f " field\fR, " \-\-field " field"
Print one field bare, for scripts:
.BR desc ,
.BR installed ,
.BR status ,
.BR severity ,
.BR cvss ,
.BR epss ,
.BR kev ,
.BR range ,
.BR record ,
.BR fixed ,
.B last\-used
or
.BR url .
A field with no value prints an empty line and exits with 0.
This option needs an ID with a package name.
.SH FILTERS
Filters narrow down
.BR "sicil report" ,
also with
.BR \-\-json .
Different filters must all match, and a repeated filter may match any of its
values:
.B \-p pam \-p libxml2 \-c 7
means pam or libxml2, with CVSS 7 or above.
.PP
A record hidden only because the filtered field is empty does not vanish:
the end of the report counts it per field, as in
.BR "3 records hidden: no CVSS" .
A filtered report says so at the top, so that
.B Red: none
is not read as an all-clear.
.TP
.BI \-p " name\fR, " \-\-package " name"
Package name.
Wildcards work, and the match is case-sensitive.
Quote wildcards, or the shell may expand them:
.BR \(aqlib*\(aq .
.TP
.BI \-\-cve " id"
CVE ID, in either case.
Wildcards work:
.BR \(aqCVE\-2021\-*\(aq .
On a kernel it lists the matching IDs.
.TP
.BI \-s " level\fR, " \-\-severity " level"
This level and above:
.BR low ,
.BR medium ,
.B high
or
.BR critical ,
in either case.
.B \-s medium
is medium, high and critical.
A severity of unknown matches only
.BR "\-s unknown" ,
so
.B \-s low \-s unknown
is every severity.
.TP
.BI \-c " score\fR, " \-\-min\-cvss " score"
This CVSS score and above.
The score is the one from the CNA, or else from CISA-ADP.
.TP
.BI \-\-unused " days"
Not used for this many days.
A package never seen in use counts as unused.
A package
.B yoklama
cannot measure is not listed and is counted as
.BR "no last-used date" .
.TP
.BI \-\-used " days"
Used within this many days.
A package
.B yoklama
cannot measure is treated as with
.BR \-\-unused .
.TP
.BI \-\-installed\-min " version\fR, " \-\-installed\-max " version"
Installed version this or newer, or this or older.
Compared by
.BR vercmp (8),
not as text.
.TP
.BI \-\-record\-min " version\fR, " \-\-record\-max " version"
Version in the tracker record this or newer, or this or older.
Compared the same way.
.TP
.BI \-\-status " status"
.B open
for red or yellow,
.B unverified
for Can\(aqt verify,
.B looks\-closed
for Looks closed.
Closed findings never match.
.PP
.B \-\-unused
and
.B \-\-used
exit with an error rather than return an empty list when
.B yoklama
is not installed, when the last run got no usage data from it,
or when
.B sicil run
has not yet upgraded the database.
A kernel has no severity, CVSS or last-use date to filter on.
.SH IDS
An ID is
.IB package : CVE\fR,
such as
.BR pam:CVE\-2025\-6020 ,
so two machines reach the same ID without seeing each other.
It carries no version, because
.B sicil show
reads the installed version when asked.
AUR findings are events and have no ID.
.PP
.B sicil show
accepts an ID or a bare CVE, which lists every package on this machine that has
that CVE.
A valid ID with no finding is not an error and exits with 0:
.B show
says the package is not installed, or that the CVE is not among the findings of
the last run, which does not prove the package is unaffected.
With
.BR \-f ,
only
.BR installed ,
.B status
and
.B url
answer for such an ID; any other field exits with 1.
.SH REPORT SECTIONS
.TP
.B Red
Official package: severity High or Critical,
or a CVE in KEV that the version range does not rule out.
AUR package: the maintainer changed hands, an orphan was adopted,
or the package left the AUR.
Kernel: a CVE covering this version is in KEV.
.TP
.B Yellow
Official package: severity Low, Medium or Unknown.
AUR package: orphan, flagged out of date, or a new co-maintainer.
Kernel: the repository has a newer version, or the branch is at end of life.
.TP
.B Removal suggestions
Packages whose removal would really take the vulnerable package off the system.
The dependency chain is walked up with
.B pactree \-ru
to the packages nothing requires
.RB ( "pacman \-Qqtt" ).
All of them must have gone unused for 60 days by the count of
.BR yoklama ,
and none may be protected or unmeasurable;
otherwise the report only shows what pulls the package in.
No suggestions are made until
.B yoklama
has observed for 60 days.
Can\(aqt verify findings, Looks closed findings and kernels never get one.
This section is left out without
.BR yoklama .
.TP
.B Can\(aqt verify
The Arch security tracker has no fix and the installed version is newer than
its record.
.BR arch\-audit (1)
reports every newer version in that case, so an old record may simply never
have been closed.
.TP
.B Looks closed
As Can\(aqt verify, but the affected range of every CVE excludes the installed
version.
A CVE counts as closed only if its record has a machine-readable version range
on a row naming the package itself, that row says everything unlisted is
unaffected, and the installed version is outside the range.
.TP
.B Kernels
One line per kernel, behind or not, with nothing to do right now.
Only
.B linux\-cachyos
and
.B linux\-cachyos\-lts
are checked.
If the package was upgraded but the machine still runs the old kernel,
the line says a reboot is pending.
.TP
.B Not found in AUR
Not on the AUR since the first time
.B sicil
looked.
.TP
.B Closed
Findings no longer open; shown with
.BR \-\-all .
.PP
One-off AUR events stay open for 7 days and then close by themselves.
Lasting states such as an orphan stay open as long as they last.
CVSS and EPSS are shown but never change a level.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
A source being down during
.B sicil run
is an expected state: it is reported as a warning and still exits with 0.
.TP
.B 1
The database cannot be opened or used, no run is recorded yet,
the database was written by a newer
.BR sicil ,
a usage filter cannot run, or
.B sicil show \-f
asked for a field of a record that does not exist.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid usage: an unknown option or value, a malformed ID,
or
.B \-f
with a bare CVE.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B COLUMNS
Overrides the terminal width that
.B report
and
.B show
fit.
.TP
.B HOME
Locates the default database.
.TP
.B PATH
Used to find the external programs, and to tell whether
.B yoklama
is installed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I \(ti/.local/state/sicil/sicil.db
The SQLite database of runs and findings.
.TP
.I \(ti/.local/state/sicil/kernel\-vulns/
Local checkout of the kernel CVE records, about 300 MB.
The first run clones it, which takes about a minute.
.TP
.IR sicil.service ", " sicil.timer
The systemd user units in the
.I systemd/
directory of the source tree.
The timer runs around 10:00 every day, within 15 minutes;
a run missed while the computer was off happens at the next start.
User timers only run while the user is logged in.
The service expects the source tree at
.IR \(ti/.daemons/sicil .
.SH CAVEATS
.IP \(bu 2
Looks closed is about upstream versions: it trusts the ranges in the CVE record.
.IP \(bu 2
Many CVE records carry no usable version range and stay in Can\(aqt verify.
Their package name may not match the product named in the record either.
.IP \(bu 2
Kernel numbers look only at the version.
CachyOS patches and the kernel configuration are not considered,
so a record may concern a module that is not even built.
The report therefore says records cover a version, not that it has that many
holes.
The count of fixes missed is a lower bound, since CVEs are often opened after
the fix.
.IP \(bu 2
The repository having a newer version is judged by the last pacman sync, because
.B sicil
needs no root and does not refresh the package lists.
.IP \(bu 2
Packages only in the CachyOS repositories are not covered, apart from the two
kernels; the Arch tracker does not know them.
.IP \(bu 2
AUR packages get no CVE check, because AUR names do not reliably match upstream
projects.
Only trust signals are collected for them.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-unused
and
.B \-\-used
say nothing about a package
.B yoklama
cannot measure, since it may be in use without being seen.
.IP \(bu 2
A short observation period makes last-use data weak:
a package listed by
.B \-\-unused
may have no date simply because
.B yoklama
has not seen it yet.
The report warns about this at the top.
.SH EXAMPLES
Check the sources now, then read the result:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sicil run
sicil report
.EE
.RE
.PP
High and critical findings on packages whose names start with lib:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sicil report \-p \(aqlib*\(aq \-s high
.EE
.RE
.PP
pam or libxml2, with CVSS 7 or above:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sicil report \-p pam \-p libxml2 \-c 7
.EE
.RE
.PP
CVEs from 2021, as JSON:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sicil report \-\-cve \(aqCVE\-2021\-*\(aq \-\-json
.EE
.RE
.PP
Open problems on packages not used for 60 days:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sicil report \-\-unused 60 \-\-status open
.EE
.RE
.PP
Installed version 5.9 or older:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sicil report \-\-installed\-max 5.9
.EE
.RE
.PP
The whole report with nothing shortened:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sicil report | less
.EE
.RE
.PP
One record, its description alone, and every package with a CVE:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sicil show pam:CVE\-2025\-6020
sicil show pam:CVE\-2025\-6020 \-f desc
sicil show CVE\-2025\-6020
.EE
.RE
